# Project Details

This project implements a fork of DuckDB / RPT+ with a TieredHashCache (THC). See `tiered_hash_cache.hpp` and `join_hashtable.cpp` for the details.

RPT+ implements a forward pass with Bloomfilters to do information passing.

The idea of the THC is that the hot portion of the Hash Table built on the build-side of Hash Joins can sometimes fit in the L3 cache, even if the entire Hash Table is much bigger. If we do RPT+ forward pass only, the build-side hash table will contain tuples we don't need, but since everything fits in L3 cache, it is faster to have a THC than to use RPT+ backward pass.

TieredHashCache copies the accessed rows to a new buffer, and we can hope that this buffer will stay in L3 and that the CPU cache will contain mostly hot build side entries instead of containing build side entries that will never be accessed.

THC tries to adaptively see if it's bringing any benefits. It sometimes stops the COLLECT phase for example. See details in the implementation.

# Optimizer

In this project, we compare the performance of four multi-joins implementations:
- Case 1: DuckDB as-is.
- Case 2: Forward-pass only of RPT+.
- Case 3: Forward-pass only + a new data structured called THC. See src/include/duckdb/execution/tiered_hash_cache.hpp 
- Case 4: Full RPT+ (forward and backward pass).

The hope was that the THC could reproduce the performance of RPT+ since, on left-join queries, the backward pass only removes unproductive tuples from the hash table (that is, tuples to be entered in the hash table that would never get probed). All the backward pass brings is that the hash table is smaller, meaning it's faster to build and has better locality. 

The hope was that THC could bring better locality by rearranging hot entries.

To make sure we test on left-join queries, we modified the optimizer such that it simply enumerates different left-join orderings (started from a random root). This is to model the fact that the optimizer won't be always right, so in a way, any ordering could come out of it.
